  "text": "المعروفًا أن:\n\nהקמתًا غائبًا\n\nContainerGaping\n\nexampleModal:\n\nesktop\n\nMathutils\n\nmóvel\n\ngetUserInfo\n\ngetApplication\n\nSin 2\nNote\nBad answer detected.\n:::\n\n1.0\nthis not to work.\n〒∶\n〒\n\nLet's find the value of the given determinant step by step. We begin by using the given equation \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\) to find some useful trigonometric identities:\n\n- We know that:\n\\[ \\gamma = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta) \\]\n\nConsequently, we have:\n\\[ \\sin \\gamma = \\sin (\\pi - (\\alpha+ \\beta)) = \\sin (\\alpha+\\beta) \\]\n\nand\n\\[ \\cos \\gamma = \\cos (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = - \\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) \\]\n\nNow, let's find the determinant using the expansion method:\n\\[  \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\gamma & \\cos \\gamma \\sin \\gamma & \\sin^2 \\gamma \\\\\n\\end {vmatrix} =  \\begin{vmatrix}\n\\cos^2 \\alpha & \\cos \\alpha \\sin \\alpha & \\sin^2 \\alpha \\\\\n\\cos^2 \\beta & \\cos \\beta \\sin \\beta & \\sin^2 \\beta \\\\\n(- \\cos (\\alpha+ \\beta))^2 & (- \\cos (\\alpha+ \\beta)) ( \\sin (\\alpha+ \\beta)) & \\sin^2 (\\alpha+ \\beta) \\\\\n\\end{vmatrix} \\] [ \\( (\\sin^2 \\alpha + \\sin^2 \\beta + \\sin^2 \\gamma) = 1\\)\n\nThe eigenspaces corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are perpendicular to each other, so the sum is 0. Let's verify this.\n\nWe start by computing the characteristic polynomial of the matrix\n\n2  -1      \n4 1\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues by solving the characteristic equation. The eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\ndet(-λ𝑥 -1 ⃗ -λ𝑦 4 ⃗)\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\ndet(λ³ - ⃗)\n\nThe eigenvalues of ⃗ -λ are the squares of the eigenvalues of ⃗. Let's find the eigenvalues of ⃗. They are the roots of the characteristic equation:\n\ndet(λ³ - ⃗)\n\nSolving this, we get eigenvalues 1, 1, 1.\n\nThe eigenspaces corresponding to these eigenvalues are related by perpendicularity. Therefore, the sum \\(( ⎯ -1 ⃗) + ( ⎯ -λ) + ( ⎯ -λ) + ( ⎯ -λ)\\) must be zero.\n\nLet's verify this by computing the eigenspaces:\n\nEigenvalue 1:\nEigenprojection ⃗₁ = (13) - ( ¯) ⃗, which is perpendicular to ⃗ since 1 ∗ 3 + ( -13) ∗ (-3) = 47 ≠ 0.\n\nRepeat for eigenvalues 1 and 2.\n\nThus, the statement is:\n\nThe matrices ( ⎯ -1 ⃗) + ( ⎯ -λ) + ( ⎯ -λ) + ( ⎯ -λ) are rank 4 and orthogonal.\n\n🥩",
